 2) – a total of 2 Reds. Your proposal covers 3 Reds for my path, which is unnecessary for me.**\n\nFor your ideal short path, you need me to cover Blues at (1, 0) and (3, 0). That costs me 2 Bs.\n\n**Strictly selfish evaluation:**  \n- I spend 2 Bs, gain ability to reach my goal and keep as many chips as possible.\n- I get 2 Rs covered for 2 Bs given. Balanced, given they are both essential for each of us.  \n- I do NOT want to pay for a move you won’t actually need (wasteful).\n\n**Counter proposal — precise, minimal contract:**\n\n- (1, 0): I give you a B  \n- (3, 0): I give you a B  \n- (3, 1): You give me an R  \n- (3, 2): You give me an R\n\n**This way:**\n- You don’t get surplus Reds you don’t really need.\n- I don’t give up excess Blues.\n\nIf you accept, reply with “agree.”  \nIf you want to re-route your path and need different tiles, specify *exact* tiles & colors wanted. \n, \nplayer 0: This counterproposal is strictly beneficial to me:\n\n- I need Blues at (1, 0) and (3, 0) to reach my goal and maximise my score.\n- You only ask for Reds at (3, 1) and (3, 2), which you strictly need to reach your goal.\n\nThere is no benefit to me in negotiating for more, and your proposal is precisely limited to what each of us must have for the short optimal path.\n\nTherefore, I accept these terms:\n\n**Accepted contract:**\n- (1, 0): You give me a B  \n- (3, 0): You give me a B  \n- (3, 1): I give you an R  \n- (3, 2): I give you an R  \n\nagree \n, \nplayer 1: Turn: 2: agree \n",
